Optional parallel nodes Add --tree-threads N to evaluate up to N open nodes concurrently; thedefault is 1, using the sequential search. Rust callers setTreeOptions::threads. The root is evaluated first, so parallel work beginsonce branching supplies multiple open nodes. Workers receive independent parent matrices, cuts, pricing state and routeidentities. Clp basis statuses are copied; other LP backends rebuild theirinternal solver state. Native model pointers and Rc snapshots never crossthreads. The coordinator merges a bounded batch in dispatch order, updatesthe incumbent and branching history, and runs strong branching and diving.New incumbents reach workers at the next batch. Parallel search may thereforevisit more nodes or select a different optimal route set than sequential search. The node limit and deadline apply to the whole tree, including dispatchednodes. Every batch finishes before returning, and unfinished nodes retaintheir lower bounds. parallel_batches reports batches containing multiplenodes. Worker count is separate from pricing threads and --master-threads;choose those budgets together to avoid oversubscribing the machine. A complete Rust entry point, requiring clp: use baldes::{
    master::{root::RootProblem, tree::{self, TreeOptions}},
    inputs::{Instance, Rounding},
};
use std::time::Duration;

fn main() -> baldes::Result<()> {
    let mut instance = Instance::read("instances/Solomon/R101.txt")?;
    instance.truncate(10)?;
    let graph = instance.graph(8, Rounding::None)?;
    let problem = RootProblem::set_partitioning(graph, instance.vehicles as f64)?;
    let mut options = TreeOptions::default();
    options.time_limit = Duration::from_secs(60);
    options.root.cg.multiplicity = (instance.customers.len() - 1) as f64;
    let result = tree::solve(problem, options)?;
    println!("{:?}: incumbent={}, bound={}",
        result.status, result.incumbent, result.bound);
    Ok(())
}
 The CLI tree uses Instance::vehicles as its fleet bound. Root-only CLI andInstance::solve_root use the customer count instead. A custom Rust callerchooses the bound in RootProblem::set_partitioning and configures thecolumn-generation multiplicity separately.

Explicit fixed partial solutions The Rust API can fix additional integer quantities of physical elementary routesin a captured node: use baldes::master::root::{self, FixedRoute};

let child = root::inherit_fixed_routes(
    parent_snapshot,
    &[FixedRoute { arcs: route_arc_ids, quantity: 1 }],
    options,
)?;
 Quantities must be positive. Paths are reconstructed and checked against graphresources and the node's pricing constraints. Calls are cumulative: fixing moreroutes in a returned snapshot retains its existing fixed part. Over-covering acustomer or violating a later branch produces an infeasible residual node.Parents and siblings retain their original row bounds, basis and fixed state. Base and inherited active cut rows are shifted by each added route contribution.The fixed elementary part uses full-memory coefficients for rank-one and strongk-path rows; remaining LP columns can retain their valid weaker limited-memorycoefficients. Separation sees fixed routes alongside residual LP routes, and newcut RHS values are shifted once. This also handles capacity/complement and mappedresource-knapsack rows. Remaining fleet multiplicity and zero-arc propagationapply to the residual routes. LP objectives, Lagrangian bounds, incumbents and enumerated MIP outcomes includethe fixed cost. RootOutput::routes and values retain their residual-columnmeaning. fixed_routes and fixed_cost expose the fixed part;RootOutput::route_values() combines it with positive residual route values.Check solver status and artificial values before accepting an LP witness.Enumerated MIP route values already include fixed routes when a witness exists. A fresh fixed-route solve discards inherited cutoff-restricted enumeration andbucket fixing. It also starts with the caller's incumbent cutoff, because aparent incumbent need not satisfy the fixed restriction. Automatic enumerationand MIP gates retain their existing behavior; fixing routes does not force aninteger solve. The API does not choose routes to fix automatically, and currentlyhas no CLI or Python entry point. The tree does not yet provide multiple pricing networks or cut callbacksduring the integer solve. Python exposes root solving; use Rust or the CLIfor the tree. Infeasible means all nodes were closed without a feasibleincumbent, under the selected bound mode and solver tolerances. Optimal uses the configured absolute and relative gap tolerances. The reportedlower bound retains the minimum node bound across open and tolerance-closedsubproblems; closing a node does not replace its lower bound with the incumbent.The final bound can therefore remain below the incumbent after successful gapclosure. If the retained bound cannot justify closure, the status is Incomplete. Vehicle-count upper branches reuse the fleet row when its coefficients matchthe branch. Repeated bounds keep the tighter RHS, while branch depth stillcounts every recorded decision. Fixed route quantities reduce that RHS once.A custom fleet row with different coefficients receives a separate branch row. The root master receives explicit root.initial_routes. Child masters inherittheir parent's restricted model and generated column pool. Routes collectedglobally for integer search, including automatic singletons and routes fromother evaluations, do not preload the child's restricted model. Equal-boundchild insertion visits the upward branch before its downward sibling. For normal tree evaluations, a compatible preceding LP can supply numericalstate after its trailing branch columns and rows are removed. Exact orderedmatrix/cost/sense/offset checks precede transfer, and the target node keeps itsown row and column bounds. Other layouts retain the independent parent fork;strong trials and dives remain isolated. Stabilization centers belong to theparent, while a tightened vehicle upper bound sets the remaining routemultiplicity. Warm metadata retains the parent's numerical and certified lower boundsseparately. Stabilization gap gating compares the selected inherited boundwith the preceding artificial-free restricted-LP boundwithin each CG round. This differs from the global integer incumbent used forpruning and enumeration. Before mutating cuts, an artificial-free completed pricing round retains acheckpoint of the master, route registry, cut/pricing state, stabilization andfixing-gap cache. A later pricing timeout that explicitly requests saved-pointrollback restores that checkpoint and preserves any improved incumbent. Childnodes start with a fresh saved-point flag. Unsaved timeouts, label limits andother errors still propagate. Recovery uses the saved pricing bound; theunfinished cut round's restricted LP objective supplies no new certificate.Failed pricing attempts remain included in the recovered node's pricing-callcount and pricing time, while its bound and warm state come from the checkpoint.

Node lifecycle Output<D>::snapshot owns the solved master, generated-column registry, rowmaps and one independent pricing solver per network. Its methods borrow theparent and return a new child or solve result; solving either child leaves theparent and sibling unchanged. Oracle counters in each output describe thatsolve, while column tags stay stable across descendants. snapshot.solve(options) resumes all networks with a new work budget. snapshot.with_row(row, coefficients) appends a shared additive row.RowCoefficients identifies a network, gives one coefficient per originalphysical arc and a once-per-route coefficient. Omitted networks contributezero. Retained master columns, the generated pool and future pricing all usethe row. Callers are responsible for the validity of supplied cuts. snapshot.fractional_assignments(tolerance) aggregates each network's use ofunit-cover shared rows. Assignment membership comes from row coefficients,so matching local arc or vertex IDs in different networks cannot alias. snapshot.branch_assignment(shared_row, network_id, assigned) builds theuse >= 1 or use <= 0 child. Assignment rows must be unit equalities withnonnegative integer contributions. Both children price every enabled network. snapshot.fix_route(route_index, quantity) fixes additional integer copiesof a registered original-feasible elementary route. It replays resources,subtracts every shared/branch/cut and multiplicity contribution, and carriesthe cost as an objective offset. Rows appended later also subtract this fixedcontribution. Output.fixed_routes reports the fixed portion separately;Output.values contains only remaining quantities. For example, after obtaining a coupled root: let candidate = root.snapshot.fractional_assignments(1e-6)?[0];
let assigned = root.snapshot.branch_assignment(
    candidate.shared_row, candidate.network, true,
)?;
let excluded = root.snapshot.branch_assignment(
    candidate.shared_row, candidate.network, false,
)?;
let assigned_result = assigned.solve(options.clone())?;
let excluded_result = excluded.solve(options.clone())?;
let restored_parent = root.snapshot.solve(options)?;
 Fresh roots still require an empty column registry; descendants own their warmmetadata internally. Pass pricing options through Options.pricing_options.Scalar resource capacity is a const generic, defaulting to two; seescalar resources.

Start with capacity and rank-one cuts cargo run --release --features clp --bin baldes -- \
  vrptw instances/Solomon/R101.txt --customers 25 --capacity-cuts --rank1 --time 60
 An experimental rank-one selection budget can limit how many active cuts touchany vertex through their support or memory: --rank1 --option BALDESrankOneCutsMaxPerVertex=8
 The default is 0 (disabled). Positive values count each cut once per vertexand graph, including both endpoints of memory arcs. Existing nonretired cutsconsume the budget across separation rounds and packing/covering passes.Candidates that exceed it are skipped in the separator's existing score order;lower-ranked candidates may fill the remaining slots. Existing cuts are retained,even if already over budget. This trades potential bound strength for cheaperpricing; 8 is an example to benchmark, not a tuned recommendation. The Rustregistry API exposes the same control as SeparationParameters::max_per_vertex. A complete Rust example: use baldes::{master::root::RootOptions, inputs::{Instance, Rounding}};

fn main() -> baldes::Result<()> {
    let mut instance = Instance::read("instances/Solomon/R101.txt")?;
    instance.truncate(25)?;
    let rounding = Rounding::None;
    let capacity = instance.capacity_cut_config(rounding, false);
    let options = RootOptions::default()
        .with_capacity_cuts(capacity)
        .with_rank_one_cuts();
    let result = instance.solve_root(8, rounding, options)?;
    println!("{:?}: root LP={}", result.master.status, result.master.lp);
    Ok(())
}
 Use capacity_cut_config(rounding, true) to attach the instance's ready/duewindows, service times, and travel matrix for Kohl separation. The CLI/Python2-path switch is for VRPTW; CVRP inputs do not supply time windows. Capacity/Kohl separation has priority 3 at the root and 1 below it. It shares apersistent subset pool. Basic cut rows may be retired by cleanup; reported cutcounts can therefore exceed the number of rows still active.

Configure or disable A complete Rust example, requiring clp: use baldes::{master::root::RootOptions, inputs::{Instance, Rounding}};

fn main() -> baldes::Result<()> {
    let mut instance = Instance::read("instances/Solomon/R101.txt")?;
    instance.truncate(10)?;
    let mut options = RootOptions::default();
    options.cg.smoothing.initial_alpha = 0.5;
    options.cg.smoothing.beta_factor = 0.0;
    options.cg.smoothing.max_updates = 10;
    let result = instance.solve_root(8, Rounding::None, options)?;
    println!("{:?}: root LP={}", result.master.status, result.master.lp);
    Ok(())
}
 Set options.cg.stabilize = false to disable it in Rust. The CLI exposes: cargo run --release --features clp --bin baldes -- \
  vrptw instances/Solomon/R101.txt --customers 10 --no-stabilization
 Python exposes the enable/disable switch through the public root API: import pybaldes

result = pybaldes.solve_root(
    "instances/Solomon/R101.txt", customers=10, stabilize=False,
)
print(result.status, result.lp)
 The Python root API does not expose the individual smoothing parameters. An unavailable best pricing solution differs from a zero subgradient. Feedbackcarries that availability explicitly; the adaptive angle retains its precedingvalue when a best solution is unavailable, matching the native early return.Completed CG rounds use the LP objective accepted within convergence tolerancefor subsequent stabilization gap bookkeeping, while the published pricing boundremains conservative. When an unavailable pricing solution accompanies an accepted pending center,the center uses raw LP duals. The saved normalized subgradient remains intact;the fallback RHS vector does not replace the direction or make it valid.Customer-only elementarity IDs and canonical retained-path export orderpreserve ordering through directional enumeration.

Controls and API Build and enable the optional tree path: cargo run --features clp,highs --bin baldes -- \
  vrptw instances/Solomon/R101.txt --customers 10 --tree --enumerated-mip
 TreeOptions::enumerated_mip = Some(MipOptions::default()) enables the samepath in Rust. It is disabled by default to preserve the existing tree schedule.A build without highs rejects this request explicitly. Disabling enumerationleaves no complete universe to send to the MIP solver. MipOptions defaults to 2,000,000 nodes, 360,000 seconds, and no objectivecutoff. The tree additionally caps time at 98% of its remaining global budget.These budgets are separate from the restricted-column heuristic'sTreeOptions::mip_node_limit / mip_time_limit. For a generic integer model, the highs feature alone is sufficient. Thiscomplete example builds a two-variable binary master: use baldes::master::{
    solvers::{highs::{self, HighsMaster}, mip::MipOptions},
    LpMaster, RowSense,
};

fn main() -> baldes::Result<()> {
    let mut master = HighsMaster::new()?;
    let row = master.add_row(RowSense::GreaterEqual, 1.5, &[])?;
    for _ in 0..2 {
        let col = master.add_column(1.0, &[(row, 1.0)])?;
        master.set_upper(col, 1.0)?;
    }
    let result = highs::solve_integer(&master, &[0, 1], &MipOptions::default())?;
    println!("{:?}: {:?}", result.status, result.solution);
    Ok(())
}
 This copies every current physical row, coefficient, cost, and upper boundthrough LpMaster. Columns have lower bound zero in this interface. Unlistedcolumns remain continuous. Integer values and all row activities are checkedbefore returning a feasible witness. Coefficients small enough for HiGHS todiscard are rejected explicitly instead of silently changing the matrix. For a routing snapshot, NodeSnapshot::solve_enumerated_mip prepares the fullroute master and applies the end-of-node route-count gate. It returns Noneif enumeration is incomplete or over the limit. This snapshot API requiresclp,highs; the generic highs::solve_integer API needs only highs.

Examples and verification Rust exposes metadata independently of constructing a graph: use baldes::{core::options::Options, pricing::Solver};

fn main() -> baldes::Result<()> {
    println!("{:?}", Options::support("BALDESlabelDominanceStrategy")?);
    let mut solver = Solver::<2>::new(3, 1)?;
    solver.set_option("BALDESlabelDominanceStrategy", 1.0)?;
    Ok(())
}
 Python exposes the same support query and runtime setter: from pybaldes import Solver

names = Solver.option_names()
implemented, scope = Solver.option_support("BALDESlabelDominanceStrategy")
solver = Solver(3, 1)
solver.set_option("BALDESlabelDominanceStrategy", 1.0)
 CLI overrides are repeatable: cargo run --release --features clp --bin baldes -- \
  vrptw instances/Solomon/R101.txt --customers 10 \
  --option BALDESlabelDominanceStrategy=1 --time 60
 OPTION_TABLE is the source of truth for names,types, defaults, and feature-dependent support. It lists only controls supportedin the current build.Bool values are numeric zero/one; integer optionsrequire integral values. Live setters also validate operation-specific modesand limits before changing solver state.

Scalar resources Documentation index · Branching Scalar capacity is a Rust const generic: Graph<const D: usize>,Solver<D>, and the labeling and enumeration engines carry the same dimension.The default is 2. Any usize count can be instantiated explicitly; there is noruntime dimension dispatch or heap-backed scalar fallback. Each scalar state,vertex window, arc consumption vector, and hot dominance cache uses a fixed array. use baldes::{Graph, Controls, price};

let mut graph = Graph::<4>::new(3, 4)?;
for vertex in 0..3 {
    for resource in 0..4 {
        graph.set_bounds(vertex, resource, 0., 10.)?;
    }
}
let first = graph.add_arc(0, 1, -2.)?;
let second = graph.add_arc(1, 2, -3.)?;
graph.arc_mut(first)?.consumption = [1., 2., 1., 3.].into();
graph.arc_mut(second)?.consumption = [2., 1., 4., 2.].into();
let result = price(&graph, &Controls::default())?;
assert_eq!(result.columns[0].resources, vec![3., 3., 5., 5.]);
# Ok::<(), baldes::core::error::OrError>(())
 The constructor's resource count is the active count, at most D. This keepszero- and one-resource models usable with capacity 2. add_resource(kind)initializes another coordinate within the compiled capacity and rejects overflow.Type annotations can use the default (let graph: Graph = Graph::new(3, 2)?;);standalone constructors should select it explicitly with Graph::<2>::new.ScalarValues<T, D> and ResourceValues<D> support indexing, slices, and arrayconversion with .into(). Exported routes contain only the active coordinates.

Rust use baldes::Graph;
use baldes::graph::coupled::CoupledResource;

let mut physical = Graph::<2>::new(4, 0)?;
for (tail, head) in [(0,1), (1,2), (2,3), (0,2), (2,1), (1,3)] {
    physical.add_arc(tail, head, -1.)?;
}
physical.elementary = true;
let deliveries = physical.arcs.iter()
    .map(|a| if a.head == 2 { 4. } else { 0. }).collect();
let pickups = physical.arcs.iter()
    .map(|a| if a.head == 1 { 4. } else { 0. }).collect();
physical.set_coupled_resources(&[
    CoupledResource::SimultaneousPickupDelivery {
        capacity: 4., deliveries, pickups,
        tolerance: baldes::graph::coupled::DEFAULT_LOAD_TOLERANCE,
    },
])?;
let result = baldes::price(&physical, &Default::default())?;
assert_eq!(result.columns[0].nodes, [0,2,1,3]);
 For an existing stateful solver, use solver.set_coupled_resources(&definitions).It keeps physical IDs and options and invalidates labels, fixing and enumeration.Reinstall after changing the physical arc vector order. Set up the complete graphbefore installing resources. Resource validation also runs during preparation. With clp, pass the attached physical graph to the ordinaryRootProblem::set_partitioning or master::mp::Network constructor.Customer covering rows and cuts continue to refer to physical customers. Noexpanded-node adapter, duplicated rows, or route projection is needed.Coupled charges are included in priced and enumerated route objectives, mastercolumn reconstruction, retained-universe repricing, fixed-route objectivevalidation and warm-start replay. Private phase I suppresses cumulative objectivecharges while retaining their state transitions and feasibility constraints.

Multiple time windows Build with --features multi-time-windows to attach a union of closedservice-start intervals to any disposable scalar resource. For example: use baldes::{Graph, Window};
let mut graph = Graph::<2>::new(3, 1)?;
graph.set_time_windows(1, 0, &[
    Window::new(8., 12.)?,
    Window::new(16., 20.)?,
])?;
 A forward arrival at 13 waits until 16. A backward latest arrival of 15 projectsto 12. Waiting is allowed between visits, including across service-window gaps;these windows constrain service starts, not the entire travel or waiting period.Service durations remain part of arc consumption, as in the single-window model. Graph::set_time_windows(vertex, resource, &[Window]) validates all intervals,sorts them and merges touching or overlapping intervals. It replaces the ordinaryvertex bound with their hull. Empty unions, nonfinite/reversed intervals, invalidIDs, and equality resources are rejected without changing the graph. A zero-widthinterval is valid. time_windows returns the normalized configured union;clear_time_windows removes it and retains the current ordinary bound. Subsequent set_bounds calls restrict the retained union. Incoming arc boundsalso intersect that union, rather than filling its gaps. An explicitly specifiedinitial source value must belong to its domain. Without one, initialization usesthe earliest permitted source value. Backward initialization uses the latestpermitted sink value within the effective bucket bounds. The feature does not add a new resource kind. Ordinary disposable dominanceremains valid because forward projection is monotone and waiting is permitted.The existing scalar resource dimensions, NG/elementarity, arc-window constraintsand resource-branch windows continue to apply.

Solving and restricting snapshots use baldes::master::{
    mp::{self, imported, MasterVariable, Options, Problem},
    preprocessing::Bounds,
    root::Row,
    RowSense,
};

let nonnegative = Bounds { lower: 0.0, upper: f64::INFINITY };
let result = imported::solve(
    Problem::<2> {
        rows: vec![Row { sense: RowSense::GreaterEqual, rhs: 1.0 }],
        networks: vec![],
    },
    vec![MasterVariable {
        id: 10, cost: 0.0, bounds: nonnegative, integer: false,
        entries: vec![(0, 1.0)],
    }],
    vec![imported::Subproblem {
        id: 7, variables: vec![10], multiplicity: nonnegative,
        columns: vec![imported::Column {
            id: 0, cost: 2.0, bounds: nonnegative, integer: true,
            entries: vec![(10, 1.0)],
        }],
    }],
    Options::default(),
)?;
let node = result.snapshot()?;
let fixed = node.with_column_bounds(7, 0, Bounds { lower: 1.0, upper: 1.0 })?;
let child = fixed.solve(Options::default())?;
 ImportedOutput.output contains ordinary solver status, bounds and routingresults. ImportedOutput.values.original contains the caller's originalvariable IDs and values. values.columns identifies each quantity by subproblemand local column ID. Internal master-variable IDs in low-level diagnostics arenot imported column IDs. Snapshots offer with_variable_bounds, with_column_bounds andwith_multiplicity. Restrictions intersect the parent's domain. Fixed columnskeep their full objective, original-vector and multiplicity contributions;they are not removed from the model. Parent and sibling snapshots remainindependent.

Incremental domain sources node.begin_import(subproblem_id) creates an immutable collection session forone existing group. Call session.collect(&ImportOptions, callback) to receivebatches from an external source. The callback receives the group definition,including all currently known columns, and the remaining time. ReturnColumnBatch { columns, complete }; set complete: true only when the sourcehas supplied its entire intended domain. All other groups retain their declareddomains. This protocol enumerates a domain; it supplies no reduced-cost pricingor external optimality certificate. use baldes::master::mp::imported::{ColumnBatch, ImportOptions, ImportStatus};

let session = node.begin_import(7)?;
let progress = session.collect(&ImportOptions::default(), |known, remaining| {
    // Enumerate additional columns using known.columns as the authoritative
    // accepted set. Respect remaining when accessing the external source.
    Ok(ColumnBatch { columns: vec![], complete: true })
})?;
if progress.status == ImportStatus::Complete {
    let solved = progress.session.solve(Options::default())?;
}
 A batch or time limit returns a resumable progress.session. Incompletesessions reject solve and inherit no finite-parent bounds or infeasibilityconclusion. Explicit envelope-covered certificate requests are described below.Completion triggers a fresh solve with all explicit restrictions replayed.Batches are validated before acceptance; duplicate IDs, invalid vectors or asource error leave the input session usable. A failed collection call does notcommit its earlier batches. External callback side effects are not rolled back;use the supplied known columns as authoritative when retrying. Time limits arechecked between synchronous callbacks, so sources must cooperate with theremaining-time argument.

Numerical oracle replies Use tree.run_with_oracles(&tree_options, &limits, callback) when an externaloptimizer can bound its entire missing-column domain without enumerating it.The callback returns ImportOracleReply containing a ColumnBatch and anoptional ImportPricingMinimum: let minimum = point.numerical_minimum(global_reduced_cost_lower_bound)?;
Ok(ImportOracleReply {
    batch: ColumnBatch { columns: generated_columns, complete: false },
    minimum: Some(minimum),
})
 The supplied number must lower-bound the reduced cost of every permittedmissing column in that group's intended domain, at the supplied objective orfeasibility prices. A minimum over a heuristic candidate list does not satisfythis contract. An optimizer may instead bound the entire domain, including knownpatterns, which is conservative. Negative infinity means unknown; positiveinfinity declares an empty missing-column domain. Neither marks enumerationcomplete. The library checks request identity and direct contradictions withreturned columns; the external optimizer supplies the global-domain guarantee. Minima are tied to the exact request, group and phase. Reusing one from an oldersolve, another group or another phase is rejected. A round queries everyunfinished group at one stored dual point before branching. for_subproblemcan retarget a point to another unfinished group without solving another LP;its clones retain the common request identity. Columns accepted during a roundare visible in the next round; the current round keeps its original prices andknown-column prefix. In numerical bound mode, all required group minima are combined with theretained-domain bound and total multiplicity caps. Existing declared envelopescan cover a group whose reply lacks a minimum. Missing coverage or an adverseminimum with unlimited quantity keeps the bound unknown. An unavailableretained-domain bound also leaves publication unknown. A positive full-domainphase-I numerical bound can close an infeasible node under that numericaloracle contract. The result's bound_mode identifies its guarantee. In certified mode, supplied numerical minima never contribute to publication orpruning. Directed envelope certificates remain required. A resumed tree cannotchange bound mode. Plain-batch run requests no numerical minima; both methodsretain previously published bounds under the unchanged bound mode. Callback, node and time limits remain resumable. A batch limit can stop partwaythrough a round; those accepted columns persist, but minima from differentrequest epochs are never mixed on resume. Allow enough callbacks to cover allunfinished groups in one round when requesting a joint numerical bound.A round that adds no columns and completes no domain either closes or branchesthe node, or returns Incomplete with an open frontier.Errors preserve the input tree, including batches accepted earlier in thatfailed invocation; callback side effects remain caller-owned.

Shared route master and column generation EvMaster<M: LpMaster> uses Baldes's existing LP backend interface. Supply anempty backend, the EV problem, its initial departure time, and an optionalnonnegative fleet limit. The master adds one set-partitioning equality percustomer and an optional fleet upper-bound row. Only complete depot-to-depotcustomer-serving routes are columns. Every inserted route is independentlyreplayed against the original problem, and its cost and customer coefficientsare recomputed. The same stop order with different charging amounts is onetravel-cost column. No seed routes are required. Phase I minimizes artificial customer coverage,pricing with zero travel cost and the current coverage/fleet duals. Whenartificial usage reaches zero to tolerance, artificials are fixed to zero andthe original travel costs are restored. Phase II prices against that objective.The two EV backends use exactly these same rows, objectives and tolerances. Before pricing, the adapter checks the LP solution's primal row feasibility,dual signs, column reduced costs, objective and primal/dual agreement. Anegative replay-validated column can improve the pool even after incompletepricing. Convergence requires an optimal restricted LP and Complete pricingwith no reduced cost below the requested tolerance. Bounded, work limits andnumerical failures cannot establish full-model LP convergence or infeasibility.Positive phase-I usage establishes numerical infeasibility only after completepricing and a positive adjusted dual objective; tolerance-sized negative reducedcosts are accounted for by decreasing customer duals. This is still f64validation, not an interval-arithmetic certificate. Results distinguish convergence, phase-I infeasibility, incomplete pricing,LP stop, iteration/time limits and numerical failures. They retain phase andpricing statistics per pricing call, plus the last validated LP snapshot. Asnapshot taken before the last column insertion is a feasible point with zeroweight on later columns; it does not claim optimality of the updated pool.travel_objective is available only when artificial coverage is zero totolerance. A restricted-pool objective is never labeled a full-model lowerbound. optimize can be called again after a work/iteration/time limit, retainingthe route pool and the current phase. The adapter is generic over LpMaster; the integration tests below use Clp.For example, with an existing problem: use baldes::extensions::evrp::{EvMaster, EvColumnGenerationOptions};
use baldes::master::clp::ClpMaster;
let mut master = EvMaster::new(problem, ClpMaster::new()?, 0.0, Some(3.0))?;
let result = master.optimize(EvColumnGenerationOptions::default())?;
// Inspect result.status before interpreting its last LP snapshot.
 LP solves receive the remaining time budget. Pricing uses its work/label limits,with elapsed time checked before and after the call; the wall-time setting isnot an interrupt inside an individual pricing operation.

API use baldes::extensions::ipm::{solve, ModelData, Options, Sense, SparseMatrix, Status};

let model = ModelData {
    a: SparseMatrix::from_triplets(1, 2, &[(0, 0, 1.), (0, 1, 1.)])?,
    b: vec![3.],
    c: vec![1., 2.],
    lower: vec![0., 0.],
    upper: vec![f64::INFINITY; 2],
    sense: vec![Sense::Equal],
};
let solution = solve(&model, &Options::default())?;
assert_eq!(solution.status, Status::Optimal);
assert!((solution.objective - 3.).abs() < 1e-6);
# Ok::<(), baldes::extensions::ipm::Error>(())
 Objectives are minimized. Matrices accept triplets or CSR input; duplicateentries are summed, columns are sorted, and explicit zeros are removed.ModelData::validate checks dimensions, finite coefficients and valid bounds.Row senses use an enum instead of numerical codes. Both original primal valuesand original row multipliers are returned. Multipliers use the conventionc - Aᵀy for reduced costs: <= row multipliers are nonpositive, and >=row multipliers are nonnegative. Options controls tolerance, iteration limit, backend, Ruiz scaling passes, andan optional cooperative wall-time budget. Status::TimeLimit preserves thedistinction from iteration exhaustion; budgets are checked between Newtoniterations, so a factorization can finish after the deadline.gap_tolerance: Some(epsilon) independently relaxes optimality usingabs(primal - dual) / (1 + abs(primal)); primal and dual feasibility still usetolerance. The default None retains the original stopping rule. The routingIPM master chooses epsilon from LP bounds during column generation, followingMunari and Gondzio (2013), Algorithm 1; see master options.SolverType::{HiPO, NormalEquations, Auto} selects the backend; HiPO is thedefault. SparseSolver, HiPOLDLT, and NormalEqLDLT also expose the linearsystems independently. Construct a new solver when matrix values or its patternchange; numerical factorize calls reuse symbolic analysis for a fixed A.Failed refactorization invalidates the previous factor. Solver::default().solve(&model, &options) provides a persistent alternativeto the one-shot solve function. Retain the Solver between related LPs: # use baldes::extensions::ipm::{ModelData, Options, Solver};
# fn example(model: &ModelData) -> baldes::extensions::ipm::Result<()> {
let mut solver = Solver::default();
let first = solver.solve(model, &Options::default())?;
let repeated = solver.solve(model, &Options::default())?;
println!("{:?}", solver.statistics());
